  "pumpingChallenge1Description": "Strings of only a's",
  "pumpingChallenge1Explanation": "This language is regular. It can be recognized by a simple automaton that accepts any number of a's.",
  "pumpingChallenge1Hint": "Think about whether a finite state machine can recognize this pattern.",
  "pumpingChallenge1Proof1": "This is a regular language because it follows a simple pattern.",
  "pumpingChallenge1Proof2": "A finite automaton can accept this by having a single state that loops on \"a\".",
  "pumpingChallenge1Proof3": "The pumping lemma condition is satisfied since we can always find strings that can be pumped.",
  "pumpingChallenge1Proof4": "For any pumping length p, we can choose x = ε, y = a^k (1 ≤ k ≤ p), z = a^(n-k) for n ≥ k.",
  "pumpingChallenge1Proof5": "Then xy^iz ∈ L for all i ≥ 0 because it's still just a's.",
  "pumpingChallenge2Description": "Strings with a's followed by b's",
  "pumpingChallenge2Explanation": "This language is regular. It can be recognized by an automaton that accepts any number of a's followed by any number of b's.",
  "pumpingChallenge2Hint": "Consider if this can be recognized by counting states or a simple state machine.",
  "pumpingChallenge2Proof1": "This language is regular because the two parts (a's and b's) are independent.",
  "pumpingChallenge2Proof2": "A finite automaton can track whether we've seen any b's yet.",
  "pumpingChallenge2Proof3": "Once a b is seen, only b's are accepted.",
  "pumpingChallenge2Proof4": "The pumping lemma is satisfied because we can pump either the a's or b's independently.",
  "pumpingChallenge3Description": "Strings with equal number of a's and b's",
  "pumpingChallenge3Explanation": "This language is not regular. For any pumping length p, the string a^p b^p can be pumped, but pumping the a's will break the balance.",
  "pumpingChallenge3Hint": "Try applying the pumping lemma with p = 2. What happens when you pump?",
  "pumpingChallenge3Proof1": "This is a classic non-regular language.",
  "pumpingChallenge3Proof2": "The pumping lemma says: for any p ≥ 1, there exists a string s = xyz where |xy| ≤ p, |y| ≥ 1, and xy^iz ∈ L for all i ≥ 0.",
  "pumpingChallenge3Proof3": "For s = a^p b^p, we can choose x = a^(p-1), y = a, z = b^p.",
  "pumpingChallenge3Proof4": "Then xy^2z = a^(p+1) b^p, which has more a's than b's, so it's not in L.",
  "pumpingChallenge3Proof5": "This shows that no finite automaton can recognize this language.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Description": "Strings with equal number of a's, b's, and c's",
  "pumpingChallenge4Explanation": "This language is not regular. It requires counting three different symbols, which cannot be done with finite memory.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Hint": "Think about how many independent counters this would require.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Proof1": "This language requires tracking three independent counters.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Proof2": "No finite state machine can keep track of three separate counts simultaneously.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Proof3": "Using the pumping lemma: choose a string with p a's, p b's, and p c's.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Proof4": "Pumping the a's will break the balance between a's, b's, and c's.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Proof5": "For s = a^p b^p c^p, choose x = a^(p-1), y = a, z = b^p c^p.",
  "pumpingChallenge4Proof6": "Then xy^2z = a^(p+1) b^p c^p ∉ L because p+1 ≠ p ≠ p.",
  "pumpingChallenge5Description": "Strings that are concatenations of a word with itself",
  "pumpingChallenge5Explanation": "This language is not regular. It requires remembering the first half of the string to match the second half, which requires unbounded memory.",
  "pumpingChallenge5Hint": "What happens if you choose a very long string and try to apply the pumping lemma?",
  "pumpingChallenge5Proof1": "This language requires remembering the entire first half of the string.",
  "pumpingChallenge5Proof2": "No matter how large the pumping length p is, we can choose w with length > p.",
  "pumpingChallenge5Proof3": "For s = ww where |w| > p, the first half has length > p.",
  "pumpingChallenge5Proof4": "The pumping lemma cannot find a suitable decomposition that preserves the property.",
  "pumpingChallenge5Proof5": "This is the language of duplicated strings, not the language of palindromes; palindromes are strings equal to their reverse.",
  "pumpingChallenge6Description": "Strings with even number of a's",
  "pumpingChallenge6Explanation": "This language is regular. It can be recognized by a finite automaton that tracks parity (even/odd number of a's).",
  "pumpingChallenge6Hint": "Think about modulo 2 instead of exact counting.",
  "pumpingChallenge6Proof1": "This is actually a regular language!",
  "pumpingChallenge6Proof2": "A 2-state automaton can track whether we've seen an even or odd number of a's.",
  "pumpingChallenge6Proof3": "Start in an \"even\" state, go to \"odd\" state on each \"a\", and back to \"even\" on the next \"a\".",
  "pumpingChallenge6Proof4": "Accept only in the \"even\" state.",
  "pumpingChallenge6Proof5": "The key insight is that we only need to track parity, not the exact count.",
  "pumpingChallenge7Description": "Union of equal a's and b's with strings of only a's",
  "pumpingChallenge7Explanation": "This language is not regular, but that cannot be proved merely by pointing to a subset. A pumping-lemma or closure-property argument is required.",
  "pumpingChallenge7Hint": "Consider what happens when you try to apply the pumping lemma to strings from the a^n b^n part.",
  "pumpingChallenge7Proof1": "This language contains both a non-regular part (a^n b^n) and a regular part (a^m).",
  "pumpingChallenge7Proof2": "The union of a non-regular language with a regular language may or may not be regular.",
  "pumpingChallenge7Proof3": "Finding a non-regular subset is not enough to prove the whole language is non-regular.",
  "pumpingChallenge7Proof4": "A valid proof can use the pumping lemma directly on strings a^p b^p from the mixed language.",
  "pumpingChallenge7Proof5": "For s = a^p b^p, the same counterexample as before applies.",
  "pumpingChallenge8Description": "Palindromes over the alphabet a,b",
  "pumpingChallenge8Explanation": "Palindromes are not regular because they require unbounded memory to verify symmetry.",
  "pumpingChallenge8Hint": "Think about what happens to the center when you pump a long palindrome.",
  "pumpingChallenge8Proof1": "Palindromes require checking that the string reads the same forwards and backwards.",
  "pumpingChallenge8Proof2": "For long palindromes, you need to remember the first half to compare with the second half.",
  "pumpingChallenge8Proof3": "Using the pumping lemma: for s = a^p b a^p, choose x = a^(p-1), y = a, z = b a^p.",
  "pumpingChallenge8Proof4": "Then xy^2z = a^(p+1) b a^p, which is not a palindrome.",
  "pumpingChallenge8Proof5": "The middle b is no longer centered properly.",
